     "citations": null,
     "text": "{\"completeness\": 0.65, \"citation_quality\": 0.3, \"severity_defensibility\": 0.75, \"overall_pass\": false, \"feedback\": \"The investigation has significant gaps that prevent it from passing triage review. First, no ATT&CK techniques are cited despite this being a classic Command and Control (C2) scenario\u2014at minimum, T1071 (Application Layer Protocol) or T1571 (Non-Standard Port) should be identified to map the attack to the MITRE framework. Second, the completeness is undermined by missing critical context: there is no analysis of whether 10.100.50.25 is a workstation or server, no mention of checking if the domain has been previously observed in your environment, and no inquiry into what triggered the DNS query (was it user behavior, malicious process, or lateral movement?). The investigation also lacks depth on the EDR investigation pathway\u2014what specific indicators (file hashes, process names, registry keys) should be prioritized during endpoint scanning? Finally, while the severity assessment correctly recognizes that the block prevented compromise, it should more explicitly address whether the mere query attempt itself warrants escalation given your organization's risk posture, and whether this host should be quarantined proactively pending EDR results rather than simply monitored.\"}",
